{"records":[{"id":"dcdy79pjcw","title":"Integrative analysis","description":"Integrative analysis of DNA methylation and gene expression through machine learning identifies diagnostic and prognostic biomarkers for stomach cancer","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-07-04T19:07:47.092Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/dcdy79pjcw","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/dcdy79pjcw"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Maryam","last_name":"Hosseini","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Maryam","last_name":"Lotfi Shahreza","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Parvaneh","last_name":"Nikpour","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Stomach Cancer","Diagnosis","Machine Learning","Prognosis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"svmj7s95bs","title":"Amplified dosage of the NKX2-1 lineage transcription factor controls its oncogenic role in lung adenocarcinoma","description":"Amplification-mediated oncogene overexpression is a critical and widespread driver event in cancer, yet our understanding of how amplification and dosage mediate oncogene regulation is limited. Here, we find that the most significant focal amplification event in lung adenocarcinoma (LUAD) targets a lineage “super-enhancer” near the NKX2-1 lineage transcription factor. The NKX2-1 super-enhancer is targeted by focal and co-amplification with NKX2-1 and controls NKX2-1 expression and regulation. We find that NKX2-1 directly controls enhancer accessibility to drive a lineage-addicted state in LUAD. We precisely map the effects of NKX2-1 dosage modulation upon both overexpression and knockdown and identify both linear and non-linear regulation by NKX2-1 dosage. We find that NKX2-1 is a widespread dependency in LUAD cell lines and that NKX2-1 confers persistence to EGFR inhibitors. Our data suggest a defining role for dosage in the oncogenic regulation of amplified NKX2-1 and that amplified NKX2-1 lineage addiction defines LUAD tumors.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-03-25T15:03:03.181Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/svmj7s95bs","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/svmj7s95bs"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"John","last_name":"Pulice","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Molecular Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"cxrsbh28df","title":"EcSeg-i: Pan-Cancer Analysis of Oncogene Amplification in Interphase Cells","description":"Dataset for figures in EcSeg-i: Pan-Cancer Analysis of Oncogene Amplification in Interphase Cells.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-05-07T03:45:38.104Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/cxrsbh28df","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/cxrsbh28df"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Utkrisht","last_name":"Rajkumar","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Computer Science","Oncology","Cytogenetics"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"p5yt2g3s7m","title":"DC-D-23-00339R2-Uncropped gel images with MW markers","description":"Uncropped gel images with MW markers of article \"The UBE2F-CRL5ASB11-DIRAS2 axis is an oncogene and tumor suppressor cascade in pancreatic cancer cells\" DC-D-23-00339R2","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-02-19T15:04:47.799Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/p5yt2g3s7m","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/p5yt2g3s7m"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Qian","last_name":"Chen","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Pancreatic Cancer","Ubiquitylation"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"8cwjcy7k34","title":"A Hybrid deep learning model for effective segmentation and classification of lung nodules from CT images","description":"Deep learning algorithms will be used to detect lung nodule anomalies at an earlier stage. The primary goal of this effort is to properly identify lung cancer, which is critical in preserving a person’s life. Lung cancer has been a source of concern for people all around the world for decades. Several researchers presented numerous issues and solutions for various stages of a computer-aided system for diagnosing lung cancer in its early stages, as well as information about lung cancer. Computer vision is one of the field of artificial intelligence this is a better way to detect and prevent the lung cancer. This study focuses on the stages involved in detecting lung tumor regions, namely pre-processing, segmentation, and classification models. An adaptive median filter is used in pre-processing to identify the noise. The work’s originality seeks to create a simple yet effective model for the rapid identification and U-net architecture based segmentation of lung nodules. This approach focuses on the identification and segmentation of lung cancer by detecting picture normalcy and abnormalities.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-10-09T13:17:58.396Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/8cwjcy7k34","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/8cwjcy7k34"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Johny Renoald","last_name":"Albert","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["3D Analysis","Lung","Computed Tomography"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"ftfvj7ct7f","title":"m6A-driven SF3B1 translation control steers splicing to direct genome integrity and leukemogenesis_Ciesla_et_al","description":"SF3B1 is the most mutated splicing factor (SF) in myelodysplastic syndromes (MDS), clonal hematopoietic disorders with variable risk of leukemic transformation. Although tumorigenic SF3B1 mutations have been extensively characterized, the role of “non-mutated” SF3B1 in cancer remains largely unresolved. Here we identify a conserved epitranscriptomic program that steers SF3B1 levels to counteract leukemogenesis. Our analysis of human and murine pre-leukemic MDS cells reveals dynamic regulation of SF3B1 protein abundance, which impacts MDS-to-leukemia progression in vivo. Mechanistically, ALKBH5-driven 5’UTR m6A demethylation fine-tunes SF3B1 translation directing splicing of central DNA repair and epigenetic regulators during transformation. This impacts genome stability and leukemia progression in vivo, supporting integrative analysis in humans that SF3B1 molecular signatures may predict mutational variability and poor prognosis. These findings highlight a post-transcriptional gene expression nexus that unveils unanticipated SF3B1-dependent cancer vulnerabilities.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-02-02T14:43:04.388Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/ftfvj7ct7f","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/ftfvj7ct7f"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Cristian","last_name":"Bellodi","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Leukemia","Myelodysplastic Syndrome","DNA Damage Response","RNA Splicing"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"n5dn4xzg7j","title":"Spatial mapping of SARS-CoV-2 and H1N1 Lung Injury Identifies Differential Transcriptional Signatures","description":"Here, we analyzed specific ARDS regions (lower left lung lobe) of interest utilizing a new spatial transcriptomic platform (Nanostring GeoMx) on autopsy-derived lung tissue from patients with SARS-CoV-2 (n=3), H1N1 (n=3), and a unique dual- infected individual (n=1). \nParaffin embedded tissues were processed and analyzed at NanoString techonology laboratories using a combination of fluorescently labeled antibodies, anti-CD68 (Santa Cruz, sc-20060 AF647, RRID: KP1), anti-EpCAM (Abcam, ab213500, RRID:EPR20532-222), anti-smooth muscle actin (Invitrogen, 53-9760-82, RRID:1A4) and the GeoMX COVID-19 Immune Response Atlas gene set with custom probe set specific for SARS-CoV-2 lung infection and tissue responses. Selection of regions of interests (ROI, 12 per patient) was performed based on the immunofluorescent viral staining, the cellular immunofluorescent profile and the pathological features of ARDS (i.e. presence of hyaline membranes and diffused alveolar damage) observed in the H&E stained sections. To ensure even and representative selection of ROIs, only patients lower left lung tissue was analyzed. Each patient had 2-3 total lung areas selected in regions of ARDS (confirmed by Dr. Benson, pathologist), 2-4 ROIs for epithelial cells (normal epithelium vs hyperplastic), 2-3 vascular beds selected, and 2-4 macrophage populations (infiltrate and clusters).  For cell-specific profiling, at least 50 cells per ROI were utilized for analyses.\nThe 1860 gene profile not only identified increased regional coagulopathy, but also robust transcriptional signatures of enhanced extracellular remodeling, alternative macrophage activation, and squamous metaplasia of type II pneumocytes in SARS- CoV-2. These gene signatures were expressed and enhanced in alveolar epithelium, vascular tissue, and lung macrophages. Both the H1N1 and dual infected transcriptome demonstrated an enhanced antiviral response compared to SARS-CoV-2.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-02-11T21:21:46.539Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/n5dn4xzg7j","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/n5dn4xzg7j"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Amit","last_name":"Gaggar","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Messenger RNA"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"w9s35sk673","title":"1H-NMR based metabolomics technology identifies potential serum biomarkers of colorectal cancer lung metastasis in a mouse model","description":"Lung metastasis is a common site of metastasis that reduces the quality of life and survival rates of colorectal cancer patients. The identification of potential diagnostic biomarkers is of great significance to treating lung metastasis in colorectal cancer. In the present study, the metabonomic profiling of serum samples of lung metastasis mice was carried out by using 1H-nuclear magnetic resonance (1H-NMR). The changes in metabolic phenotypes during the progression of lung metastasis of colorectal cancer were noticeable, and some metabolites were identified.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-10-27T12:58:27.03Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/w9s35sk673","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/w9s35sk673"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yan","last_name":"Li","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Metastasis","Metabolite","Colorectal Cancer"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"vc3tck4y6p","title":"Supplementary files for Integrative transcriptome and methylome analysis reveals characteristics of BVDV-persistently infected calves and identifies biomarkers","description":"Supplemental Material for “Integrative Transcriptome and Methylome Analysis Reveals Characteristics of BVDV-Persistently Infected Calves and Identifies Biomarkers”. This supplementary file contains three supplementary figures and five supplementary tables.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-10-23T08:16:00.572Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/vc3tck4y6p","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/vc3tck4y6p"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"jiaho","last_name":"wang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Animal Genetics"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"45fvfkvcxv","title":"A systemic metabolome atlas of pan-lung cancer delineates convergent metabolic signatures and subtype-specific vulnerabilities","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-06-03T19:03:34.356Z","embargo_date":"2027-06-02T23:59:59Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/45fvfkvcxv","data_type":"dataset","access":"embargoed","doi":["10.17632/45fvfkvcxv"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Ping","last_name":"Du","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Li","last_name":"Zhejin","orcid":null},{"first_name":"He","last_name":"Tian","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Peng","last_name":"Yang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Peilin","last_name":"Jia","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Zhaohui","last_name":"Tong","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Chunguo","last_name":"Jiang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sin Man","last_name":"Lam","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Guanghou","last_name":"Shui","orcid":null}]}],"total_records":132777,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":102,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":132777,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":132055,"restricted":68,"embargoed":654},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2248},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":669},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Centre","count":467},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":419},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":414},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":403},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":396},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":330},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":267},{"id":"https://ror.org/05r0vyz12","name":"Ministerio de Ciencia, Innovación y Universidades","count":222}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":130150},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data